WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.172 --> 00:00:01.845
(creepy music)

00:00:01.845 --> 00:00:02.896
- [Joanna] I think people
now are more afraid

00:00:02.896 --> 00:00:04.128
of death than they've ever been

00:00:04.128 --> 00:00:05.429
because they're not familiar with it.

00:00:05.429 --> 00:00:07.711
Because it seems like this exotic other.

00:00:07.711 --> 00:00:09.458
If every single person who has ever lived

00:00:09.458 --> 00:00:12.625
has died, it can't be that bad, right?

00:00:14.752 --> 00:00:16.207
I can't imagine how one wouldn't be

00:00:16.207 --> 00:00:17.874
fascinated by death.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:22.796
My name is Joanna Ebenstein.

00:00:22.796 --> 00:00:23.861
I'm the Creative Director and co-founder

00:00:23.861 --> 00:00:26.416
of the Morbid Anatomy Museum.

00:00:26.416 --> 00:00:28.461
The name Morbid Anatomy seems to suggest

00:00:28.461 --> 00:00:29.906
to a lot of people something more like

00:00:29.906 --> 00:00:31.627
the American Morbidity Museum

00:00:31.627 --> 00:00:33.210
in American Horror Story.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.124
And so, people will come in and be like,

00:00:35.124 --> 00:00:36.056
"Where are the dead babies?"

00:00:36.056 --> 00:00:36.889
And we're like,

00:00:36.889 --> 00:00:38.124
"No, we don't have dead babies here."

00:00:38.124 --> 00:00:39.375
(upbeat music)

00:00:39.375 --> 00:00:40.533
There's two parts in the museum:

00:00:40.533 --> 00:00:42.735
There's the temporary
exhibition which is always

00:00:42.735 --> 00:00:45.009
borrowed from different
private collectors.

00:00:45.009 --> 00:00:46.136
And there's this room we're in which is

00:00:46.136 --> 00:00:48.376
the Morbid Anatomy Library.

00:00:48.376 --> 00:00:50.765
I think a lot of people
who like this project

00:00:50.765 --> 00:00:52.812
are people who had to
choose at a certain point

00:00:52.812 --> 00:00:54.544
between art and science.

00:00:54.544 --> 00:00:55.767
I always wanted to be a scientist

00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:57.052
and I wanted to be an artist.

00:00:57.052 --> 00:00:59.261
So I'm always interested and drawn to

00:00:59.261 --> 00:01:01.915
artifacts from time
periods before those things

00:01:01.915 --> 00:01:03.043
were divided up.

00:01:03.043 --> 00:01:05.568
These ideas that death and beauty,

00:01:05.568 --> 00:01:07.263
art and science, cute and horrific,

00:01:07.263 --> 00:01:09.034
have to be separate.

00:01:09.034 --> 00:01:10.626
When I was a kid I loved animals.

00:01:10.626 --> 00:01:13.255
And I loved live animals, I loved my cat.

00:01:13.255 --> 00:01:15.667
But also I loved looking at animals

00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:17.258
at the Natural History Museum that were

00:01:17.258 --> 00:01:18.658
in a jar in formaldehyde.

00:01:18.658 --> 00:01:19.889
My college friends would call me morbid

00:01:19.889 --> 00:01:21.662
because I liked to pick up roadkill

00:01:21.662 --> 00:01:23.757
and preserve the skins.

00:01:23.757 --> 00:01:25.637
I never stopped doing it.

00:01:25.637 --> 00:01:27.328
But I was cognizant of
the fact that people

00:01:27.328 --> 00:01:29.367
thought I was a freak.

00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:30.958
Really through starting the museum,

00:01:30.958 --> 00:01:32.613
that's when I really discovered there were

00:01:32.613 --> 00:01:33.859
so many more people like me

00:01:33.859 --> 00:01:35.738
than I ever suspected.

00:01:35.738 --> 00:01:38.238
(light music)

